orecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Tennessee remains one of the most reliably Republican states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of fourteen points toward the GOP. The 2028 race is an open contest with a term-limited incumbent leaving the White House, and neither side has a named nominee here. No public polls of the Tennessee presidential race, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices are available, so the outlook rests on the state's underlying partisanship.\n\nSurrounding coverage offers little reason to expect a deviation. A September poll showed Trump's approval underwater in Tennessee, but that survey concerned the governor's race and does not directly measure the presidential contest. Republicans start with a clear advantage; Democrats would need a substantial national shift to make the state competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0603, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1012, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.11, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1055, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0961, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1038, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0969, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1086, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.082, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0965, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1008, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.0993, "margin": -14.0}]}